{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-383a4016-dcd9-4799-a773-7ee080dd8689","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1c9ac4a02aa2865","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9016-cz58lmf1/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9016-E001.md","content":"# Workbench last-activity data source investigation for console reporting\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation determines how the notebook controller's per-workbench\nlast-activity tracking can be consumed by the RHOAI web console for the\nper-project idle-workbench report. The console's only interface to component\nbackends is the Kubernetes API (no private component APIs), while the notebook\ncontroller tracks last activity per kernel through Jupyter server API probes\ndriven by its culler. Whether that tracked value is readable today through a\nKubernetes-API surface (for example, a Notebook CR status field or an\nequivalent readable resource) — or whether the controller must first expose it\n— decides the shape of the downstream console reporting work and whether\nnotebook-controller code changes are needed at all.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Locate, in the notebook-controller source and configuration, where\n  per-workbench last-activity timestamps are stored and updated (activity\n  probes, culler config, any existing status or annotation writes).\n- Determine whether that data is currently readable through the Kubernetes API\n  by a console consumer, without private APIs or in-process access.\n- Enumerate the viable consumption paths for a console report (existing\n  readable surface, controller-side exposure of the value, or another\n  Kubernetes-readable mechanism), assessing each against the console's\n  Kubernetes-API-only interface constraint.\n- Recommend one path, with a concrete pass/fail criterion the reporting\n  implementation can test (a named field or surface to read).\n- If the data is not readable today, specify what controller-side change would\n  expose it and the impact on the console report's scope and sequencing.\n\n## Outcomes\n\nTwo bounded outcomes:\n\n- **Outcome A — the data is already readable via the Kubernetes API**: the\n  console report reads the existing surface; no notebook-controller changes.\n- **Outcome B — the data is not readable today**: notebook-controller work is\n  required to expose per-workbench last-activity on a Kubernetes-readable\n  surface; the conditional branch plan for that work is attached to this\n  investigation's outcome.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written findings report states, with source-level evidence, where\n  per-workbench last-activity is stored and whether it is readable through the\n  Kubernetes API today.\n- Every candidate consumption path is assessed against the console's\n  Kubernetes-API-only interface constraint, with the assessment recorded in the\n  findings.\n- The recommendation names a concrete, testable data source (field or surface)\n  for the idle-workbench report, or — if none exists — the specific\n  controller-side change required to create one.\n- The outcome (A or B) is recorded so the reporting work and any conditional\n  controller-side work can be scheduled from it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **HLR-1 (P1)** — enables: resolves the data-source unknown behind the\n  per-project idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources)\n  sourced from the notebook controller's activity tracking.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":45268,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99f844a42ff9bb63","agent_id":"af98b122f29171fcb","session_id":"66891df0-ced6-412e-9fab-8ee1943bcf3f","uuid":"216838db-3dc3-4c5d-9251-c4be6ac0b210","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:22:13.936Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9016"}
