dency=0 vs E003's=1 with identical \"none\" rationales — that's a plain inconsistency. E003's rationale even starts with \"None —\". E002's says \"No upstream contribution or vendor coordination\". Same condition, different values. One of them is mis-calibrated. This is exactly \"1-2 have arguable values\" → Criterion 5 = 1? Or is it a minor issue?\n\nHmm, the scoring rubric maps: minor issues alone don't reduce the score, but 3+ minors in the same criterion cost 1 point. And \"1: ...1-2 have arguable values\". So whether I call it \"arguable value\" (→ score 1) or \"minor issue\" (1 minor → no reduction), I need to decide.\n\nLet me count what I'd flag as minor in Criterion 5:\n1. E002 external_dependency=0 while E003 scores the identical \"no external dependency\" condition +1 — inconsistent calibration between sibling epics.\n2. E002/E003/BE005 human_process_gates=0 with \"no approval step\" rationales — rubric's +1 condition is \"None\".\n\nIf I flag #2 as three separate minors (one per epic), that's 4 minors → 3+ minors cost 1 point → Criterion 5 = 1. If I treat #2 as one calibration convention applied consistently (arguable), and #1 as one arguable value, then \"1-2 arguable values\" → Criterion 5 = 1.\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider #2 once more. Is there any place where the skill clarifies 0 for human_process_gates? Let me search the decompose prompt for more signal guidance, and also check how revise-agent or SKILL.md treats this. Actually, let me look at the full Step 6 in decompose-agent.md around the signals to see if there's more guidance on when to use 0.\n\nAlso — I should double check the ai_implementability values: E002 has \"ai_implementability: High, ai_implementability_score: 3\" — wait, the decompose prompt says \"Do not include ai_implementability or ai_implementability_score — the pipeline computes those\". But the epic files have them. Hmm — the frontmatter.py set commands may have added them, or the pipeline ran. Not my concern: the