{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d1256e2e9b2bd20","type":"tool_result","content":"124:10. `konflux-onboarding` ∥ general implementation parallel (config independent of code; AC gates first execution). **Add NO blocking edge in either direction.** It is tempting to make the general implementation depend on `konflux-onboarding` (\"the code needs a build pipeline first\") — do not. That coupling is enforced by the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" AC on the general implementation epic, never by a DAG dependency. A `konflux-onboarding → general` edge serializes work that must run in parallel and is a rule violation. This holds for EVERY `konflux-onboarding` × general-implementation pair in the decomposition, whatever the general epic's component or deliverable — a stack-wide assembly, packaging, or evidence epic that consumes the pipeline's outputs is still a general implementation. There is no \"different component\" or \"genuine artifact dependency\" exemption: if the coupling is real, express it as an AC on the consuming epic (\"the signed attestations from the build pipeline are included and validated\"), never as an edge.\n129:13. Implementation producing artifact another epic's code builds against (API, CRD, library) → consuming Implementation serial. Does NOT apply to configuration references (image digests, endpoint URLs) — those are AC gates. **In particular, an image digest / SHA consumed by OLM/CSV packaging (`RELATED_IMAGE`) is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact: the packaging epic gets a \"pinned image digest available\" AC, NOT a `dependencies` edge on the image-build or `konflux-onboarding` epic.** Wiring such an edge both mis-serializes parallel work and violates Rule 10 — the digest is filled in at execution time, so the epics run in parallel. The same holds for any build-pipeline output another epic consumes as CONTENT — signed attestations, SBOMs, provenance, scan results: the consuming epic gets an AC (\"the evidence package includes the signed attestations produced by the build pipeline\"), not an edge. Code builds against an API, CRD, or library; it does not build against attestations or digests.\n330:Add optional fields only when non-null: `implementation_type=<value>`, `branch=<value>`, `gated_by=<epic_id>`, `gate_failure_impact.action=<value> gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"<text>\"`. Every epic that depends on an Investigation should have `gated_by` pointing to that Investigation (see Rule 3).\n349:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), and implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead); and work-type-extracted enforcement deliverables (a general Implementation epic titled as a lint rule / CI check / static-analysis check / validation harness that shares its component with another non-docs Implementation epic — fold the enforcement work back into every epic whose surface it covers, as Scope plus a verification AC, and delete the standalone epic). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb77dc3f908c610b","agent_id":"ade07d90fd8e81ae9","session_id":"66891df0-ced6-412e-9fab-8ee1943bcf3f","uuid":"63009